<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/509-bce">509 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Temple of Jupiter Capitolinus, Aedes Iovis Optimi Maximi Capitolini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus, or Temple of Jupiter Capitolinus, was the most important temple of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a>. Traditionally dedicated in 509 BCE, the first temple, built of wood with the help of Etruscan specialists, was the largest in the Italian world, though its exact size is disputed. Roman tradition credits the kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquinius Superbus</a> with its foundations and building. It burned in 83 BCE and was rebuilt by 69 BCE; after further fires the fourth temple stood until the fall of the empire. Only parts of its foundations and podium survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-jupiter-optimus-maximus.jpg" alt="Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hiro-o at Japanese Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TempleofCapitoliumRome.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to Livy, the <b>Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus</b> on Rome's Capitoline Hill was dedicated on the Ides of September in the Republic's first year, by the consul Marcus Horatius Pulvillus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-13">13 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
